      {
        "id": "p17-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Differentiate $f(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{x}$ and show that $f'(x)>0$ for all $x$ in its domain.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=\\dfrac{3}{x^2}>0$ for every $x\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=-3x^{-1}\\\\f'(x)&=3x^{-2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{3}{x^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor every $x\\ne0$, $x^2>0$, so $f'(x)>0$ throughout its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain why $f(-1)>f(2)$, despite $f'(x)>0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There is a vertical asymptote at $x=0$. The function increases separately on $(-\\infty,0)$ and $(0,\\infty)$; $-1$ and $2$ lie on different branches.",
          "worked": "$$f(-1)=3,\\qquad f(2)=-\\frac32.$$\nThe vertical asymptote at $x=0$ separates the domain into two intervals. A positive derivative shows that the function increases on each interval separately; it does not compare values across the asymptote.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      }
    ]
  },
